<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/age-of-revolutions">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Between 1688 and 1948 the basic unit of political life changed from the <em>subject</em> to
    the <em>citizen</em>. The engine was a stack of ideas about who rules and why — consent,
    rights, nations — and each revolution stress-tested one. Some succeeded, several devoured
    their children; all of them spread their vocabulary worldwide.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two and a half centuries of overturns — scrub and watch the ideas travel continents" widget="timeline" config={revTimeline} />

  <h2>Reading the pattern</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ideas migrate.</strong> Locke's Two Treatises (1689) → Jefferson's Declaration (1776) → the French Declaration of Man (1789) → Latin American constitutions. Each draft borrows and radicalises the last.</li>
    <li><strong>Revolutions widen in circles.</strong> Who counts? First property-holding men, then (slowly, unevenly) the propertyless, then women, then colonies. New Zealand extended the vote to women in 1893; the franchise is still being argued over today.</li>
    <li><strong>Successful revolutions build institutions, not just crowds.</strong> America survived because of a constitution and courts; France's first three attempts each collapsed into strongman rule before the third republic stuck.</li>
    <li><strong>The modern state is the residue:</strong> defined borders, equal citizens under law, national economies — and the wars of the twentieth century were, grimly, the sorting mechanism.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hobsbawm, The Age of Revolution; Encyclopædia Britannica; UN Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948).</p>
